  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 131 AM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nTuesday and Wednesday...\n\nUpper level northwest flow will remain over the area, which will \ncontinue to drive bouts of energy through the region. Long term \nguidance is in general agreement on a stronger wave passing through \nIowa Tuesday night into Wednesday, dragging a cold front through the \narea. A look at model soundings would indicate that the environment \nahead of it will be sufficient for some strong to severe storms. \nShear will generally be between 30-40 KTs with modest instability, \nfavoring the chance for some organized convection. A look at the \nenvironment and overall pattern would favor the potential for an \novernight MCS to potentially impact the area. Main hazards with this \nwould be severe wind gusts, with heavy rain and frequent lightning. \nThe SPC is also highlighting much of the area in a Slight Risk,\nor level 2/5, for severe weather. There remains some \nuncertainty on timing and convective coverage, owing to \ndifferences amongst guidance. As more short term guidance comes \nin over the coming days, we will be able to hone in on some of \nthe finer details. \n\nBeyond...\n\nNorthwest flow will remain through the remainder of the week, as a \nridge builds over the Central CONUS and guides another shortwave \nthrough the area on Thursday into Friday. Moisture will remain \nlimited, which should limit precipitation chances. Some guidance \nbrings in overnight PoPs, as a weak LLJ converges into the wave. \nAlthough, PoPs are largely <20% area-wide. Thus, much of the area \nshould remain dry going into Friday. The main wave with better \nforcing will pass through during the day of Friday, bringing low-end \nPoPs in our north. In the end, much of the area will remain dry \nthrough this stretch. The weather is currently geared to be quite \nnice to end the week, with temperatures in the upper 70s, a mix of \nclouds/sun, and comfortable humidity.",
